## 4. State machines

### 4.1 Node epistemic status (§4.2, the machine the envelope must not contradict)

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> candidate: ingest / authored_node (§6.1b — always lands here)
    candidate --> canonical: evidence binding (R14 / ×5)
    candidate --> refuted: falsifier observed (R15)
    canonical --> superseded: a newer node supersedes it (§4.4 Temporal)
    canonical --> refuted: falsifier observed (R15)
    superseded --> [*]: RETAINED
    refuted --> [*]: RETAINED — high-value knowledge (✓1, FPL-2)

    note right of candidate
      Forbidden edge (no arrow exists):
      candidate --> canonical on frequency
      thresholds (5+ refs / 3+ domains).
      Frequency may raise review priority;
      it may never constitute promotion (x5).
    end note

    note left of refuted
      No state has an exit to deletion.
      "Nothing is ever deleted; supersession
      only" is expressed as the ABSENCE of a
      terminal delete transition and the
      ABSENCE of a `deleted` enum member.
    end note
```

**What the envelope schema can and cannot enforce.** A JSON Schema validates one document,
never a transition. Layer 1 enforces the **per-state predicate** — the properties a node in
each state must satisfy (R12, R14, R15) — which is what makes an illegal transition produce an
illegal *document*: a frequency-promoted node reaches `canonical` with `evidence: []` and is
rejected by R14. The transition *relation itself* (that this node was `candidate` yesterday)
is unrepresentable here and is owned by **`I-STORE`**; recorded as gap **GAP-1** (§9).

### 4.2 Confidence lifecycle (Δ6)

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Absent: authored_node — confidence property FORBIDDEN
    Absent --> Derived: store computes on admission (stored_node)
    Derived --> Derived: recomputed on evidence change,\npropagated along derived_from / supports (§4.2, IT-05)
    Derived --> [*]
    note right of Absent
      This is the entire schema-level content of
      "DERIVED, never authored": a writer literally
      cannot express a confidence at the boundary.
      Policy would be unenforceable; absence is structural.
    end note
```

### 4.3 Fixture verdict decision flow

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Load
    Load --> SelectProfile: manifest.profile
    SelectProfile --> Layer1
    Layer1 --> Layer2: no errors
    Layer1 --> CheckExpectation: errors
    Layer2 --> CheckExpectation
    CheckExpectation --> Pass: dir==valid AND clean
    CheckExpectation --> Pass: dir==invalid AND rejected at manifest.layer\nAND error path == expect_error_path
    CheckExpectation --> Fail: valid fixture rejected
    CheckExpectation --> Fail: invalid fixture accepted
    CheckExpectation --> Fail: invalid fixture rejected for the WRONG reason
    Pass --> [*]
    Fail --> [*]
```

The third `Fail` edge is the one that gives the corpus its value; a harness without it
degrades to "some JSON is bad", which is not a conformance test.

