## 9. Perspectives covered (L09 multi-perspective critique input — 7 stances)

- **Builder.** The schema skeleton (§2.3), the 9 canonical regexes, the 33-row fixture table
  (§5) and the 8-test contract (§6) are transcribable without further design judgement. Every
  fixture is defined as *one mutation of a named valid sibling*, so authoring is mechanical. ✔
- **Architect.** Nothing here is invented: every field traces to a §4.2 line, every enum to a
  §4.3/×5 enumeration, every tightening is listed in §8 with its clause (CER-6 satisfied — this
  refines constitutional intent, it does not create it). The one closed set not literally
  enumerated in §4.2 (`EvidenceRef.kind`) is drawn verbatim from ×5 and flagged as OQ-3. ✔
- **Risk Officer.** RK-1/RK-2/RK-3 are named with owners and blast radius; the two debt
  candidates carry CER-3 fields and repayment conditions. The largest risk (S-PROBE falsifying
  the field set) is localised by DD-1 to one `required` list. ✔
- **Performance.** 33 documents × 2 layers, in-process, no I/O beyond 35 small file reads.
  Sub-second; irrelevant to any budget. The `summary` ≤ 280 bound is itself the FPL-4 context
  economy control at the node level (Ω3) and is boundary-tested (V06, I09). ✔
- **Security.** (a) `additionalProperties: false` everywhere means an ingest source cannot
  smuggle unknown fields (tombstones, inline payloads, confidence) past the boundary — I05 and
  I14 prove it. (b) `EvidenceRef` cannot carry an artifact, so SAKG structurally cannot mint
  evidence (§3.3.3, the third non-authority guarantee). (c) The two frequency-promotion
  fixtures (I02, I03) attack the ×5 poisoning surface from both sides — missing evidence and
  laundered evidence. (d) X1 (I17) is a tenancy attack: a node claiming one namespace in its
  `id` and another in its field would defeat Ω5 isolation at the storage boundary. (e) Fixtures
  contain no real hashes, URIs, or namespaces. ✔
- **Replay / determinism.** UTC-`Z`-only timestamps (DD-3, I12), lowercase-hex sha256 (R10,
  I13), `uniqueItems` on evidence and coordinate values, and a URN `$id` that no validator will
  dereference over the network — together these keep any two validations of the same corpus
  byte-identical, which `corpus_hash` (§5.1 r3) depends on. ✔
- **Maintainer.** One schema file, one manifest, one test module. T4 (bijection) and T5
  (coverage closure) mean the corpus cannot silently rot: adding a fixture without a manifest
  entry fails, and dropping the last fixture for any type/status/kind fails. Every rule has an
  id (R1–R16, X1–X4) that appears in the schema, the manifest, and the failure message. ✔

**Decorrelation (K3 / A-08).** F-ENVELOPE is a `fable` node; every consumer of these fixtures
(`I-STORE`, `I-INGEST`) is `sonnet`. The corpus is authored *against* an implementation that
does not exist yet and by a different provenance than the one that will be graded by it. The
fixtures must therefore be written as attacks, not as illustrations — §5.2 is ordered that way.

